WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
 Okay, so we've taken a look at loops and now we're gonna just take a little bit of a look

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:08.060
 at some of the operations that you can do with loops.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:13.900
 Python has this, as we'll soon learn, object-oriented approach to its operators and the plus can

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:19.440
 add strings and it can add numbers, floating-point numbers, integer numbers, strings, et cetera.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:23.740
 And so the plus similarly works this way with lists.

00:00:23.740 --> 00:00:28.000
 The plus looks to its left and looks to its right and says, "What am I adding?"

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:31.660
 And in the case that I'm adding the list one, two, three, and the list four, five, six,

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:32.900
 it concatenates them together.

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.100
 And this way it sort of functions like a string and so we get one, two, three, four, five, six.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:40.660
 It just concatenate this list to another list.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:44.940
 And it doesn't change A or B just like in any kind of assignment statement.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.100
 Calculations on the right side don't change the variables and they produce a new variable

00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:52.860
 and then assign that into C.

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:57.100
 You can also use list slicing and it's easy to remember.

00:00:57.100 --> 00:01:00.900
 If you remember how strings work, list works exactly the same way.

00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:03.460
 So of course it's a little tricky.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:05.160
 The first number is the starting position.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.340
 They start at zero.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:08.380
 So one is right there.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:10.520
 So it's the zero position, the one position.

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:12.420
 Start at one, right?

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:14.580
 But go up two but not including three.

00:01:14.580 --> 00:01:17.460
 There's one, two, three.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:20.100
 So this goes up two but not including three.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.620
 And that's why we get 41, 12 out of that.

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:24.060
 So up two but not including.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:27.040
 I'll just say that over and over and over again.

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:30.140
 If we do, you can leave the first part out.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.220
 You can leave the first part out here and you can say, up two but not including four.

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:37.720
 So that starts at the beginning, goes up two but not including four.

00:01:37.720 --> 00:01:40.360
 And so that's how we get that piece right there.

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:46.680
 We can say start at the position three, zero, one, two, three.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:49.140
 Start at position three and go to the end.

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:53.380
 Now the fact that the number three is in here is sort of irrelevant.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:56.540
 Three to the end is those three numbers.

00:01:56.540 --> 00:01:58.780
 And then you can do the whole list with slicing as well.

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:02.940
 Again, these pretty much are the exact same examples I used when I was doing strings.

00:02:02.940 --> 00:02:03.900
 They're pretty much the same.

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:09.500
 There's a number of different methods and

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.700
 you can look up all the documentation in list.

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.660
 I often just use the dir command to remind myself of them.

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:17.060
 Append will look at.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:19.900
 Count looks for certain values in the list.

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:22.060
 Extend adds things to the end of the list.

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:24.220
 Index looks things up in the list.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.020
 Insert allows them, the list to sort of be expanded in the middle.

00:02:28.020 --> 00:02:29.980
 Pop pulls things off the top.

00:02:29.980 --> 00:02:32.300
 Remove removes an item in the middle.

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:39.300
 Reverse flips the order of them and sort, puts them sorted order based on the values.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.940
 So let's look at a couple of these.

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:50.220
 So if we build a list from scratch, we have a way to ask for an empty list.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:52.940
 There are a couple different ways to ask for an empty list.

00:02:52.940 --> 00:02:55.780
 We could use just two square brackets next to each other, but

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:59.700
 this is a form we call the constructor form where we say, hey Python, make a list.

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:03.420
 In this case, the word list is like a reserved word to Python.

00:03:03.420 --> 00:03:09.980
 It's really a reserved class, but say list parenthesis says make me an empty list.

00:03:09.980 --> 00:03:12.300
 And then assign that list into stuff.

00:03:12.300 --> 00:03:18.420
 So stuff is now, it's a list object, it's of type list, but it has nothing in it.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.580
 And then we can call the append method, stuff.append, and stick book in.

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.340
 And then we say, and that knows how long, the stuff knows how long it is,

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:29.900
 where the end is and how to add something to it, and then add a 99 to it.

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.960
 And we print it out, and we got book a 99.

00:03:31.960 --> 00:03:36.900
 Reminding ourselves that lists, while they're often the same types of variables,

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:39.900
 same types of values in the various positions in the list,

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.180
 it doesn't always have to be that way.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:45.580
 Then we say, we'll stuff that append cookie, you can keep on going.

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:48.740
 And then we end up with three things and the cookie.

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:56.320
 We have an in operator, it works pretty much like the in operator in a string.

00:03:56.320 --> 00:03:57.820
 Is nine in my list?

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.460
 And that's pretty simple and the answer of course is yes, nine is in my list.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.940
 Is 15 in my list?

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:06.540
 Looking through, no it's not, 15 is not in my list.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.020
 And then there's the not in operator.

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:10.180
 You can think of that as kind of like one operator.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:11.900
 Is 20 not in the list?

00:04:11.900 --> 00:04:14.180
 And the answer is since it's not there is true.

00:04:14.180 --> 00:04:17.380
 And so that's a way to just, you know, it's kind of like starts with or

00:04:17.380 --> 00:04:20.780
 ends for strings, same kind of stuff.

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:23.220
 Lists are in order and they're sortable.

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:27.060
 And so this is something that we take good advantage of.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.140
 A lot of what computers want to do is sort stuff.

00:04:29.140 --> 00:04:32.900
 You know, look all these things up, append them, and then get them sorted.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:38.420
 And so there is this method inside of list, that's just the sort method.

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:43.380
 So here we put three values in 0, 1, 2 positions, 0, 1, and 2, Joseph, Glenn, and

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:44.340
 Sally.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:47.420
 And then we tell the list to sort itself, and then we print it out.

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:49.620
 Now, this is actually sort of the list in place,

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:51.620
 which is different than upper and lower.

00:04:51.620 --> 00:04:55.860
 Cuz if you remember, strings are not mutable, but lists are mutable.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:04:58.700
 And so you say, hey, just sort yourself.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:01.540
 Okay, and so just sort yourself, and then it sorts it.

00:05:01.540 --> 00:05:04.420
 And then it's in alphabetic order, Glenn, Joseph, and Sally.

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:05.820
 I happen to be clever.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:08.180
 I only put strings in there, and I put my uppercase and

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.220
 lowercase in a very consistent pattern.

00:05:10.220 --> 00:05:11.660
 But the list has changed.

00:05:11.660 --> 00:05:15.460
 And if I look at list sub 1, that is the second item,

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.780
 which is Joseph, that prints out right down there.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:24.460
 There's a whole bunch of built-in functions to help manipulate list.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:28.780
 The other things I was showing was sort is a method that's part of list, but

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:32.740
 there are other functions that take list as their arguments.

00:05:32.740 --> 00:05:36.500
 We already talked about the len function, tells you how many items there are.

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:41.220
 There is pretty obvious max, it says go through and find the largest.

00:05:41.220 --> 00:05:44.020
 Min, go through and find the smallest.

00:05:44.020 --> 00:05:46.300
 Sum goes through, adds them all up.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:52.340
 And we can say let's do average by taking the sum of all of them and

00:05:52.340 --> 00:05:53.460
 dividing it by the length.

00:05:53.460 --> 00:05:57.300
 And you might think to yourself, wow, I wish we'd have known this a few chapters

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.380
 back when we were having to write all those loops to do max, min, sum, largest,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.020
 smallest, etc.

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:05.740
 You can kind of think in your mind that inside each one of these functions is

00:06:05.740 --> 00:06:08.300
 a loop that does pretty much what you did in those chapters.

00:06:08.300 --> 00:06:10.820
 And part of the reason we did that back then,

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:14.060
 even though these things were here, was they're kind of easy loops to understand.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:17.780
 And so those are there.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:25.140
 And basically, there allows two different ways of building loops to do the maximum

00:06:25.140 --> 00:06:25.900
 minimum.

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:33.180
 Now, it's not necessarily all that much easier to do something using these,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:38.660
 because you either can do them the old way, or you can make a list and

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:39.700
 then use these functions.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:41.500
 So let's take a look, and

00:06:41.500 --> 00:06:45.620
 I'll just say that these two bits of code are doing the exact same thing.

00:06:45.620 --> 00:06:49.260
 And what they are is they're implementing a program that's gonna repeatedly ask for

00:06:49.260 --> 00:06:53.100
 numbers until we type the word done, and then it's gonna compute the average and

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:54.580
 tell us what they are.

00:06:54.580 --> 00:06:59.100
 And so using sort of the stuff from the loop chapter,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:02.420
 we start with a total variable and account variable, set them to zero.

00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:06.820
 And then we read a number, we check for done to break out, but

00:07:06.820 --> 00:07:09.300
 then we convert it to a floating point value.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:12.300
 And then we say total equals total plus value and count equals count plus one.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:14.980
 And so this is gonna run over and over and over again,

00:07:14.980 --> 00:07:16.900
 however many times we're gonna do this.

00:07:16.900 --> 00:07:21.020
 And then it's gonna pop out, and when it's done, it's gonna have this value of total.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:24.500
 The running total will become the overall total divided by count, and

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:26.780
 it'll print the average out, okay?

00:07:26.780 --> 00:07:30.700
 And so that's kinda how we would have done this before we knew how to do this

00:07:30.700 --> 00:07:32.140
 with lists.

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:34.180
 Now, let's take a look at the other one.

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:38.340
 In the other one, we say let's make an empty list.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:41.660
 Remember, this is that constructor syntax that says to Python,

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:44.980
 make me an empty list, and assign the empty list.

00:07:44.980 --> 00:07:46.460
 It has nothing in it, right?

00:07:46.460 --> 00:07:50.020
 But it is a list, has nothing in it, into the variable numList.

00:07:50.020 --> 00:07:51.900
 Now we're gonna write another loop.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:54.580
 This part here is the same, these three lines.

00:07:54.580 --> 00:07:57.780
 Read the number, if it's done, quit, and convert it to value.

00:07:57.780 --> 00:08:00.020
 But instead of doing the actual calculation right now,

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:01.820
 what we're gonna do is just append it to the list.

00:08:01.820 --> 00:08:05.100
 So the list will start out empty, then the three will be in the list,

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:07.980
 then the nine will be in the list, then the five will be in the list.

00:08:07.980 --> 00:08:11.860
 So we're appending each time through the loop, we're appending into the list.

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:15.420
 So we're just growing the list every time I read a value,

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:18.380
 instead of actually computing something with the value that we've got.

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:23.540
 So in either case, we get value, and in one case, we append it to the list.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:27.700
 And then finally, it finishes, the break happens, and then we just say, hey Python,

00:08:27.700 --> 00:08:30.300
 sum up everything in the list, add these three numbers together, and

00:08:30.300 --> 00:08:34.420
 then divide it by the length of all those things, and you'll have the average.

00:08:34.420 --> 00:08:39.740
 And so these two things give us exactly the same output.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:40.980
 Now, there is one difference.

00:08:40.980 --> 00:08:45.300
 If there was like one million or one billion numbers,

00:08:45.300 --> 00:08:47.900
 they actually have to all be stored in the memory simultaneously.

00:08:47.900 --> 00:08:51.820
 Whereas here, it's actually doing the calculation of the billion numbers and

00:08:51.820 --> 00:08:53.580
 not using up so much memory.

00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:56.900
 For most of the things that you're gonna be doing, the difference in memory,

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:58.060
 there is a difference in memory.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:03.980
 This one here uses more memory, but I can't draw very well.

00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:10.820
 It uses more memory, but it doesn't really matter by the time it's all said and done.

00:09:10.820 --> 00:09:16.380
 And so for you, the difference between these things is not all that significant,

00:09:16.380 --> 00:09:19.860
 but it's important to understand that they're just two techniques to accomplish

00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:21.340
 the same thing with lists.

00:09:21.340 --> 00:09:31.340
 [MUSIC]

